{
  "id": "Compound::DB00424 - Gene::1131",
  "verbalization": "Hyoscyamine, a muscarinic acetylcholine receptor antagonist, is implicated in the relationship with Chrm3 through a series of interconnected molecular and functional pathways. The evidence suggests that Hyoscyamine binds to Chrm2, which is involved in the activation of G-protein coupled neurotransmitter receptor activity, and this activity is further linked to Chrm3. Similarly, Hyoscyamine binds to Chrm2, which is also associated with acetylcholine receptor activity, leading to the participation of Chrm3 in this process. The presence of a lowest common ancestor between Hyoscyamine and Atropine, which is an antimuscarinic agent, further supports a potential mechanism involving muscarinic receptor modulation. The integration of these pathways highlights a possible role for Hyoscyamine in modulating muscarinic receptor activity, which in turn influences the function of Chrm3. The evidence suggests a functional link between Hyoscyamine and Chrm3 through the activation of muscarinic receptors and their downstream signaling pathways.",
